WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.920
YUFENG GUO: The
TensorFlow graph is

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.540
a source of pride, performance,
and frustration among users.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.850
How can we keep the pride
and the performance,

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.530
but without the frustration?

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.790
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.220
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.610
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.010
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.010
we're going to check
out how to smooth over

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:34.310
some of those pain points that
exist in using the TensorFlow

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.900
graph.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.670
TensorFlow has been
long associated

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.380
with the computational graph.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.990
After all, the library
is all about tensors

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.240
that flow through a graph.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.420
Yet this can be a source of
great frustration for folks

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.520
who are just starting out.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.350
This is mostly
because everything

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.050
is part of the graph,
and I mean everything,

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.960
from the numbers that are
used to represent your models

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.030
to the operations themselves.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.300
Everything is part of the graph.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.920
This can lead to
various difficulties

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.710
when it comes time to,
say, debug your TensorFlow

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.240
code since the development model
can feel like write everything

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:12.860
perfectly and then run it.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.470
You wrote it all
perfectly, right?

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.440
And of course programming
should not operate like this.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.400
It would be great to be
able to iterate on things,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.900
see partial results,
and peer into the graph

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.150
to be able to print out
more meaningful outputs

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.860
than just tensor MatMul0
shape 2, 2 dtype float 32.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.560
What does that mean?

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.810
But how can you ignore
the deferred execution

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.790
process of the graph?

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.620
This is the goal of Eager mode.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.320
TensorFlow will eagerly
execute your operations,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.900
thus allowing you to see
the results right as you're

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.570
working, rather than
waiting until everything

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:52.550
is coded up and
connected before you can

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.370
see if anything is done right.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.260
Let's take a look at
this basic example

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.840
where I'm multiplying
some numbers together.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.360
When we haven't yet
enabled Eager mode,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.490
things work the
way we are used to.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.140
Printing out a
TensorFlow variable

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.960
gives some informational
text about the operation

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.610
and the expected dimensions.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.070
But to actually
execute that node,

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.490
we'll need to do a bit more
boilerplate code by wrapping it

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:18.620
in a session and then
running that session

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.720
to pull out the values.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.210
This is just way too
much code to just try

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:24.740
to look at some numbers.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.340
Now let's try that same code,
but we'll enable Eager mode

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.550
by adding
tf.enable_eager_execution

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:35.570
as the very first thing that
is run after import tensorflow.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.130
And we'll look at what happens.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:40.790
The printed results, they
show exactly the value

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.470
of the matrix multiplication.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.560
That's what's great
about Eager mode.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.630
It enables you to use
TensorFlow more efficiently

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.600
without losing prior
functionality or the features

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.650
that you're used to.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.130
So much of software development
lives in the trial and error

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.150
process in running some code,
seeing what errors you get

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:01.250
back, and changing that code.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.220
So the sooner you can test
the code and see the errors,

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.310
the more efficient and
effective you can be.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.250
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.130
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.110
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.045
when they come out.

00:03:16.045 --> 00:03:18.320
For now, try out Eager mode.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.650
See if it's right for you.

